implies the printed one. Lean status per paper is kept in authored/evidence-status.json.","citation_ids":["cite.long.prop-finite-state-nogo"],"evidence_ids":["mapping.long.prop-finite-state-nogo"],"id":"long.prop-finite-state-nogo","kind":"claim","label":"Long record Proposition 10.9 (A finite-state restriction for the stated pulse family)","locator":"Long record Proposition 10.9, p. 117, label prop:finite-state-nogo","source_id":"paper.long.pdf","statement":"*For a “balanced pulse” family at location $m\\ge2$ (radius $\\rho=\\lfloor(m{+}1)/2\\rfloor$, parameters $0\\le r\\le\\rho$, moving mass between positions $m$ and $m{+}1$ with the weighted total $2c(m)+c(m{+}1)=2\\rho$ of the two coefficients fixed), if a predecessor state is constant across the whole family, then no function $\\mathrm{decode}:\\mathrm{State}\\to\\mathbb N$ can recover the parameter $r$ from $\\mathrm{state}(r)$ for every $r$. The family has exactly $\\rho+1=\\lfloor(m{+}1)/2\\rfloor+1$ members, so the fan-out is unbounded in $m$. More strongly, any finite set of states carrying an exact decoder for the family has at least $\\rho+1$ elements, unbounded in $m$.*\n\n***Scope.** Excludes bounded-state encodings of pre-$m$ history that must distinguish every member of the displayed balanced-pulse family, whether the coefficients are $\\varphi$ or a Möbius-support indicator. Applying it to \\#249 or \\#257 requires showing that the relevant orbit realises that family, and it does not rule out every finite-state proof strategy. **Object or representation:** about a *representation* class, the encodings of history that separate the balanced-pulse family; it says nothing about $C$ or $\\mathcal A$ directly.*","status":"unverified"},"ref":"sha256:264b08973a5f23b2f98ad989b0c1708096513d86929f19048623a2a9fe18092c/nodes/long.prop-finite-state-nogo","relations":[{"derived":false,"family":"arguments","from":"sha256:264b08973a5f23b2f98ad989b0c1708096513d86929f19048623a2a9fe18092c/nodes/long.prop-finite-state-